rtc: light and deep sleep on a timer, ESP32, C3 and S3

ESP-IDF enters sleep by setting RTC_CNTL_STATE0's SLEEP_EN, then spins
until the chip powers down (deep sleep) or RTC_CNTL raises SLP_WAKEUP
(light sleep). None of the RTC_CNTL models handled it: light sleep never
returned and deep sleep spun until a watchdog reset the chip, with the
reset reason and lost RTC memory of a watchdog reset. The library's
battery sketches went round in reboots.

Each chip's RTC_CNTL now keeps the registers the sleep code writes and
reads back (SLP_TIMER0/1, STATE0, WAKEUP_STATE, the wake-up cause, INT_*,
DIG_PWC), and on SLEEP_EN arms a timer for the wake-up time when the timer
wake-up is enabled, converting RTC slow-clock ticks to virtual time.

- Light sleep: the timer raises SLP_WAKEUP and the wake-up cause.
- Deep sleep (DIG_PWC's DG_WRAP_PD_EN set): the CPUs are held and the
  digital domain is reset, which stops the app's watchdogs; RTC memory
  and RTC registers are kept. When the timer fires the chip boots through
  the ROM with reset reason DEEPSLEEP_RESET and wake-up cause "timer".
  ESP32: CPUs held through RTC_CNTL's cpu-stall lines, and waking resets
  the digital domain again (its flash boot watchdog had been counting
  while the CPUs were held), releasing them when that reset is done.
  S3: the machine's cpu-stall input was empty; it now holds a CPU for
  deep sleep and releases only a hold it set. C3: one system reset at
  entry, with the CPU held halted (start_powered_off) until the timer
  fires; the C3 model also gains RTC time (TIME_UPDATE, TIME_LOW0/HIGH0),
  which it lacked.
- The RTC memory CRC unit, which the ROM and ESP-IDF run around deep sleep
  to decide whether to run the wake stub (RTC_CNTL 0x100/0x104 on the
  ESP32, SYSTEM_RTC_FASTMEM_CONFIG/CRC on the C3), finishes at once with
  a fixed result. Unmodelled, deep sleep hung before SLEEP_EN.

Any other reset (power-on, the app's reset, a watchdog) ends sleep. Only
the timer wakes the chip: GPIO, touch and ULP wake-ups are not modelled.

